  </div>

  <NoSignupStatement />

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       3. PLAIN-LANGUAGE EXPLANATION
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section class="ss-section" aria-labelledby="explanation-heading">
    <h2 id="explanation-heading">What the Social Security Break-Even Age Means — and Why It Matters</h2>

    <p>
      Choosing when to claim Social Security is one of the most important financial decisions of retirement. The <strong>social security break-even calculator</strong> helps you evaluate the trade-off between getting <em>fewer larger checks later</em> versus <em>more smaller checks earlier</em>.
    </p>

    <h3>The Core Claiming Trade-Off</h3>
    <p>
      Under Social Security Administration (SSA) rules:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Claiming at Age 62 (Earliest):</strong> You get monthly checks 5 to 8 years sooner, but your monthly payout is permanently reduced by <strong>30%</strong> (for those with an FRA of 67).</li>
      <li><strong>Claiming at Full Retirement Age (67):</strong> You receive <strong>100%</strong> of your Primary Insurance Amount (PIA).</li>
      <li><strong>Waiting until Age 70 (Maximum Delay):</strong> You earn 8% per year in Delayed Retirement Credits, resulting in a monthly check <strong>24% larger</strong> than FRA (or 77% larger than claiming at 62).</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="ss-info-box">
      <p class="ss-info-box-heading">Key Takeaway: The "Longevity Rule of Thumb"</p>
      <p>
        If you live past <strong>Age 80</strong>, delaying Social Security until Age 70 almost always yields the highest total lifetime dollar payout. If you expect a average or long lifespan (into your 80s or 90s), waiting pays off significantly.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       4. WORKED EXAMPLE (STEP BY STEP)
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section class="ss-section ss-section--tinted" aria-labelledby="worked-example-heading">
    <h2 id="worked-example-heading">Step-by-Step Worked Example: David's $2,000 Benefit Decision</h2>
    <p>
      Here is how the <strong>social security breakeven age</strong> is calculated for a 62-year-old retiree.
    </p>

    <div class="ss-example-card">
      <div class="ss-example-scenario">
        <span class="ss-example-label">Scenario</span>
        <p>
          <strong>David</strong> is turning 62 and has a Full Retirement Age (FRA 67) benefit of <strong>$2,000/month</strong>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <ol class="ss-example-steps">
        <li>
          <strong>Monthly Check Rates:</strong><br />
          - Age 62: $1,400/month (70% of PIA)<br />
          - FRA 67: $2,000/month (100% of PIA)<br />
          - Age 70: $2,480/month (124% of PIA)
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Cumulative Payout at Age 75:</strong><br />
          - Claim at 62: $218,400 collected (13 years of checks)<br />
          - Claim at 67: $192,000 collected (8 years of checks)<br />
          - Claim at 70: $148,800 collected (5 years of checks)<br />
          <em>At age 75, claiming early at 62 is ahead.</em>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>The Crossover (Break-Even Age 80):</strong><br />
          By <strong>Age 80</strong>, total collected by waiting to 70 reaches <strong>$297,600</strong>, surpassing the $302,400 collected by claiming at 62.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Cumulative Payout at Age 85:</strong><br />
          - Claim at 62: $386,400 total<br />
          - Claim at 67: $432,000 total (+$45,600 bonus)<br />
          - Claim at 70: $446,400 total (+$60,000 bonus)<br />
          <em>By age 85, waiting until 70 yields $60,000 more in total income.</em>
        </li>
      </ol>

      <div style="background: #fff8f0; border-left: 4px solid #E8761A; border-radius: 0 0.5rem 0.5rem 0; padding: 1rem 1.25rem; margin-top: 1.25rem;">
        <p style="font-size: 0.875rem; font-weight: 700; color: #E8761A; margin: 0 0 0.25rem 0;">💡 Pro Tip</p>
        <p style="font-size: 1rem; color: #0D2137; margin: 0; line-height: 1.6;">Screenshot your break-even results and bring them to a Social Security office appointment or a free SHIP counseling session. Break-even math often shifts when you factor in spousal survivor benefits — a factor this calculator does not model.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       5. FAQ SECTION (FAQPage SCHEMA)
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section class="ss-section" aria-labelledby="pitfalls-heading">
    <h2 id="pitfalls-heading">Common Social Security Break-Even Mistakes and How to Avoid Them</h2>

    <h3>Using Your Current Salary Instead of Your Career Average</h3>
    <p>The SSA averages your highest 35 earning years to calculate your benefit. Entering your current salary overestimates your base benefit if your earlier career years were lower-paid.</p>
    <p>The fix is <a href="https://ssa.gov/myaccount" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" style="color: #0A3D3A; text-decoration: underline;">log into My Social Security at ssa.gov/myaccount for your official estimated benefit at each claiming age</a>.</p>

    <h3>Ignoring Spousal or Survivor Benefits in the Break-Even Calculation</h3>
    <p>For couples, the higher earner's decision to delay creates a larger survivor benefit for the lower-earning spouse. The break-even analysis for a couple is very different from that of a single retiree.</p>
    <p>The fix is use the couples mode in this calculator and consider modeling the survivor scenario.</p>

    <h3>Assuming Break-Even Age Is the Only Factor</h3>
    <p>Break-even age ignores cash flow needs, tax bracket management, and the earned income limit before FRA. Some retirees need the income at 62 regardless of break-even math.</p>
    <p>The fix is treat break-even as one of three factors; the others are your health/longevity outlook and your monthly cash flow needs.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="ss-section" aria-labelledby="faq-heading">
